\chapter{Vocabulary and Practical Investigations}
\label{app:glossary}

\section{A working vocabulary}

\begin{description}
\item[ABI] An application binary interface: calling conventions,
representations, executable and linking expectations, and related
agreements needed by compiled programs.
\item[API] An application programming interface. Shared API behavior can
support source portability without providing binary compatibility.
\item[Buffer] Storage whose contents have a temporary role in a transfer
or computation. Correctness includes ownership and reuse timing.
\item[Capability] An authority-bearing reference or, in Linux's specific
usage, one of the powers split from traditional superuser authority.
The meanings should be distinguished in context.
\item[Compositor] A component that arranges and presents surfaces,
often combining them into an output image. Its other responsibilities
depend on the display architecture.
\item[Context switch] A transition between execution contexts that
preserves enough state to resume suspended work.
\item[Copy-on-write] Sharing storage until a modification requires a
private copy, with protection and lifetime rules enforcing the illusion.
\item[Descriptor] A process-local integer reference to an open kernel
object or description. It is not a pathname.
\item[Directory] A namespace object associating component names with
filesystem objects or identifiers.
\item[Distribution] An integrated, maintained assembly of a kernel,
libraries, packages, configuration, and release policies.
\item[Driver] Code implementing a hardware or device interface.
Graphics stacks often contain both kernel and userspace driver components.
\item[End-of-file] A result defined by the object being read.
For a pipe, an empty buffer becomes EOF when no writers remain.
\item[Exec] Replacement of a process's program image. A successful exec
is not creation of a new process identity.
\item[Fork] Creation of a child process with defined inheritance from
its parent. Physical copying strategy is an implementation choice.
\item[Free software] Software licensed to preserve specified user
freedoms, including studying, modifying, and redistributing it.
\item[Inode] A filesystem representation of an object's metadata and
content mapping. A directory name and an inode are different entities.
\item[Interrupt] An event that redirects execution under hardware rules,
commonly to report a device or timer event.
\item[Kernel] The privileged core managing protected access to resources
and implementing essential system mechanisms.
\item[Namespace] A context in which names are interpreted.
Linux namespaces virtualize selected views of system resources.
\item[Open file description] Kernel state associated with an opening,
including properties such as offset, potentially shared by descriptors.
\item[Page fault] An access that cannot complete under current mapping
rules; it may invoke a useful allocation policy or report an invalid access.
\item[Pipe] A byte-stream communication object with finite buffering
and defined endpoint lifetimes.
\item[POSIX] A family of specifications for portable operating-system
interfaces and associated environments, with versioned scope.
\item[Process] An execution environment containing identity, memory,
resources, and execution state; distinct from the program it executes.
\item[Pseudoterminal] A kernel-provided terminal pair enabling emulators
and remote services to supply terminal semantics to applications.
\item[Race] Behavior dependent on an ordering not adequately controlled
by the program's protocol. Not every race is a language-level data race.
\item[Signal] A notification mechanism with defined default actions,
handling rules, masking, and pending-state behavior.
\item[Socket] A communication endpoint whose operations depend on its
address family, type, protocol, and state.
\item[System call] A controlled request for kernel service through an
architecture-specific entry mechanism.
\item[Terminal emulator] A program interpreting terminal protocols and
presenting their character-oriented interaction in another environment.
\item[Transaction] A unit of work with explicitly defined atomicity
and recovery properties. The word alone does not establish those properties.
\item[Virtual memory] The mapping and management mechanisms separating
program-visible addresses from physical storage and enforcing access rules.
\item[Zombie] A terminated process's retained status record awaiting
collection, rather than a process still executing instructions.
\end{description}

\section{Investigate a pipeline without large data}

The following shell fragment creates an isolated temporary directory,
counts a tiny ASCII dataset, and removes its own files on exit.
It assumes a Unix-like environment with \texttt{mktemp -d} and a
POSIX-style shell. The mktemp invocation is common but should be checked
on unusual systems. No administrative privileges are needed.

\begin{verbatim}
#!/bin/sh
set -eu
scratch_dir=$(mktemp -d)
trap 'rm -rf "$scratch_dir"' EXIT
printf '%s\n' 'red blue red' 'blue green' \
    > "$scratch_dir/words"
LC_ALL=C tr -cs 'a-z' '\n' < "$scratch_dir/words" |
    sort | uniq -c | sort -nr
\end{verbatim}

The counts should be two red, two blue, and one green.
The order of the tied entries follows the final sort's rules; it is not
an additional semantic property of word counting. The sample begins with
a letter, avoiding an initial empty token. Its letter-only definition is
intentional, not a recommendation for processing arbitrary natural language.

Now run each stage separately and inspect the intermediate stream.
Ask where records begin and end, which stage assumes equal values are
adjacent, and why the final order differs from alphabetical order.
This turns a pipeline from a memorized spell into an inspectable algorithm.

The trap's variable is assigned before use and quoted during cleanup.
The shell's error mode still does not establish complete pipeline-error
handling for every shell version. For maintained automation, specify the
shell and failure policy deliberately rather than generalizing from this
small controlled input.

\section{Observe an unlinked open file}

This Python 3 example uses the Unix ability to keep an open file after its
name is removed. It creates and removes only its own temporary file.
It is intended for Unix-like systems; other operating systems can use
different deletion semantics.

\begin{verbatim}
import os
import tempfile

with tempfile.TemporaryDirectory() as directory:
    path = os.path.join(directory, "example")
    with open(path, "w+b") as stream:
        stream.write(b"still available\n")
        stream.flush()
        os.unlink(path)
        assert not os.path.exists(path)
        stream.seek(0)
        assert stream.read() == b"still available\n"
\end{verbatim}

The assertions distinguish namespace state from open-object state.
The name is gone, but the descriptor still refers to the object.
After the final close, the filesystem can reclaim storage under its
normal lifetime rules. This is not a crash-durability experiment and
should not be described as one.

\section{Compare one shared offset with two independent openings}

The next example demonstrates an open file description directly.
Python's unbuffered descriptor operations avoid adding a language-level
read buffer that could obscure the offset being examined.

\begin{verbatim}
import os
import tempfile

with tempfile.TemporaryDirectory() as directory:
    path = os.path.join(directory, "letters")
    with open(path, "wb") as stream:
        stream.write(b"abcd")
    first = os.open(path, os.O_RDONLY)
    duplicate = os.dup(first)
    independent = os.open(path, os.O_RDONLY)
    try:
        assert os.read(first, 1) == b"a"
        assert os.read(duplicate, 1) == b"b"
        assert os.read(independent, 1) == b"a"
    finally:
        for descriptor in (first, duplicate, independent):
            os.close(descriptor)
\end{verbatim}

The duplicate shares an offset with the first descriptor.
The independent opening starts from its own offset.
The same underlying bytes participate in both relationships.
Fork inheritance can produce a related sharing effect across processes,
but duplication lets this investigation isolate the essential distinction
without adding scheduling.

\section{Read a teaching kernel by invariant}

With an authorized copy of Sixth Edition source or an xv6 source tree
matching the supplied book, select one operation rather than reading
random functions. For a pipe read, identify the condition that means data
is available, the state that records writers, and the lock or interrupt
protocol protecting both.

Write down what must remain true while the reader sleeps.
Then trace what the writer changes before it wakes the reader.
Ask whether an intervening notification can be lost and which exact
operation closes that window. Finally inspect how the last writer's close
changes the condition for EOF.

For a filesystem operation, make the same exercise about persistence.
List every block changed by a file creation, identify the commit point
in the supplied xv6 log, and consider a crash before and after it.
The exercise should end with a statement of assumptions, especially what
a disk write is assumed to preserve.

These investigations are deliberately small.
Their purpose is to train distinctions that survive changes of hardware,
language, and user interface. A reader who can explain the observations
has learned more than the syntax of a particular command.
